id: k8s.rollback
kind: kubernetes
title: Rollback
aliases: rollout undo, revert deployment
tags: operations, deployment
questions:
  - a deploy broke production, how do you recover?
  - users report 500s after a deploy — do you roll back or fix forward?
invariant: Revision history holds previous Pod templates, so a rollback is a template change the Deployment controller applies again.
answer_30s: `kubectl rollout undo` switches the Deployment back to a previous ReplicaSet template, and it is itself a rollout, so the same readiness gates apply.
answer_2m: |
  Rollback is fast because the old ReplicaSet still exists; it is limited
  because it only reverts the template. Anything the bad version already did to
  shared state -- a schema migration, a message format, a cache key change --
  stays applied. That is the honest answer to the follow-up question: rollout
  undo is not a transaction, so database changes need backward-compatible
  migrations (expand/contract) to keep rollback real. `revisionHistoryLimit`
  decides how far back you can go.
when_to_use:
  - a bad template or bad image in production
commands:
  - kubectl rollout history deployment/api => which revisions exist and their change cause
  - kubectl rollout undo deployment/api => revert to the previous template
  - kubectl rollout status deployment/api => confirm the reverted revision is fully healthy
failure_modes:
  - rolling back code while leaving an incompatible migration applied
  - revisionHistoryLimit too small, so the needed revision is gone
related:
  - k8s.deployment.rolling-update
  - k8s.deployment
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/#rolling-back-a-deployment
